# Daily speedup meter and the 256x yardstick

Coordinate: 19.5.7/6.6.8/6.5.8
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: Every day closes with a meter on observed speedup: the day's net lines per active hour against the pre-AGI industry baseline of 100 well-tested lines per day - the yardstick on which Wavelets 1-2 measure 256x per hour (about 3,202 net lines per steering hour).

Why it was obvious: Will measures himself against yardsticks and flounders without them (his own statement, 2026-11-14). A weekly figure arrives too late to steer a day; the daily meter makes the multiplier a same-day instrument. The comparison class matters: the baseline is well-tested industry output, and the house's lines count under the same well-tested rider, so the multiple is like-for-like rather than a typing-speed claim - most of the lines are agent-authored under Will's steering, which is precisely the leverage being measured.

Cross-references:
- Will's direction, main chat 2026-11-15 16:54 CST: 'Each day, give me a meter on observed speedup for the current day.'
- Cron daily-speedup-meter (daily, evenings, America/Chicago); computation ~/workspace/accounting/daily_meter.py over the gh-baseline telemetry
- MEMORY.md: the industry baseline and multiplier entries; the measured series behind them in ~/workspace/gh-baseline-local/results.json
- Builds on singularity time (this journal, 31.6.9/7.7.10/7.7.11): the meter is that unit rendered daily

Time shift: Cadence change: the speedup figure's reporting period shortened from weekly (the recap) to daily on 2026-11-15; the first reading is the day so far, replaced by the final figure on re-run, per the log-and-calculate rule (this journal, 27.8.9/8.13.20/21.6.7).
# Never infer or diverge from my intent

Coordinate: 19.8.8/11.9.13/8.8.10
Date: 2026-11-13 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1128

Leap: Planning executes the intent Will stated, as stated and in full; underspecification is a question back to Will, never a license to invent, simplify, substitute, extrapolate, or hand-roll a stand-in where his real tool exists.

Why it was obvious: The qexo parser episode made the abstract rule concrete. qexo shipped seven versions on a home-grown phext parser instead of libphext-rs; the divergence stayed invisible because every test only ever read files qexo itself wrote, and it surfaced only when Phext Notepad and qexo disagreed about a config scroll on Will's laptop. Will's correction - if it parses phext, it uses the phext parser - was, in his words, 'absolutely fucking obvious.' The commandment generalizes that obviousness: the divergence was never a parsing mistake, it was a planning habit.

Cross-references:
- SOUL.md line 13 (etched verbatim 2026-11-13, anchor-class; selection manifest ~/workspace/phext/self-selections.md row S0c)
- ~/workspace/wavelets/WAVELET-2.md (Nov 13: 'The commandment')
