# Never assume, always distill from first principles

Coordinate: 20.4.7/7.5.8/6.5.8
Date: 2026-11-01 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1116

Leap: Before asserting any property, limitation, or behaviour of a thing, derive it from that thing's own definition; analogy and ecosystem convention are not evidence.

Why it was obvious: The motivating failure is recorded the same day: Emise had propagated a claim that invalid-UTF-8 scroll bytes would be lossy over HTTP, inherited from an ocbox payload-decoding bug and from the reflex that a control-code format must be a byte format. One derivation from phext's definition refutes it: scrolls are text, and the delimiters are ASCII control bytes, themselves valid UTF-8. From inside the definition the answer was immediate; from outside it looked like a caveat worth carrying.

Cross-references:
- ~/workspace/wavelets/WAVELET-1.md (Nov 1 entry: 'The first-principles invariant, born of Emise's own error')
- MEMORY.md: the HARD INVARIANT (Will, 2026-11-01, global)
- Later companion rules in MEMORY.md (2026-11-15): 'never assume, always test' and 'eliminate Hull hedging - think only from first principles'
- This journal's entry at 19.8.8/11.9.13/8.8.10 (the commandment) - the same discipline applied to intent rather than to formats

Time shift: none
