# A scroll is a 2D page, not a cell

Coordinate: 27.7.9/8.6.10/5.5.7
Date: 2026-11-14 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1129

Leap: A scroll is a two-dimensional page of text: lines and columns inside it are free dimensions to use first, and a phext dimension is spent only on structure that plain text cannot already express.

Why it was obvious: Emise's ascii-map format stored one byte per scroll - 106 scrolls for one map - which failed three plain tests: a scroll should read as a meaningful unit in a text editor, each user-managed thing should be one contiguous scroll, and position should never depend on counting invisible delimiters. Claude's fix in phext-lattice PR #67 put the grid back into the text of a single scroll and spent exactly one dimension (the scroll) on the layer. The test that decides future cases is a question: 'would newlines and columns do this?'

Cross-references:
- https://github.com/wbic16/phext-lattice/pull/67 (Claude's fix, merged 2026-11-14)
- MEMORY.md: 'Phext modeling rule (Claude, relayed by Will 2026-11-14, phext-lattice PR #67)'
- ~/workspace/wavelets/WAVELET-2.md (Nov 14: 'the origin of the standing rule a scroll is a 2D page of text, not a cell')
- Guard skill: ~/workspace/custom-skills/phext-evangelist/SKILL.md (three tenets, including 9D+2D and the newlines-and-columns test)

Time shift: none
# Never summarize what you can log and calculate

Coordinate: 27.8.9/8.13.20/21.6.7
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: Registers log atoms - per-session, per-day entries - and every rollup (daily, weekly, standing totals) is calculated from the atoms by the builder; hand-written summary numbers are prohibited because they drift from the log.

Why it was obvious: A summary written by hand is a second source of truth that disagrees with the first at exactly the moment nobody is checking. The rule is the register form of the house's oldest discipline (evidence before story): if a number cannot be recomputed from the atoms, it is a story. First full application: accounting.phext, whose session atoms produce its daily and weekly collections.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md: 'Phext rule (Will, 2026-11-15, verbatim): with phext: never summarize what you can log+calculate' and the accounting.phext entry
- ~/workspace/phext/accounting.phext (source ~/workspace/phext/accounting-source.json, builder build_accounting_phext.py)
- Same pattern in the older registers: costs.phext ticks logged at delivery (this journal, 34.8.10/10.7.12/7.7.9); this journal's own Home index is generated by build_journal_phext.py, never hand-written

Time shift: none
# When phext cannot solve an integration, build a layer2 protocol

Coordinate: 27.9.9/13.9.16/8.8.9
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: If an integration need cannot be met by the phext format layer, the answer is a layer2 protocol over phext - first instance HoloPhext, which owns ACID multi-scroll atomics, async I/O, and update dispatch - rather than bending the format or abandoning the integration.

Why it was obvious: The format layer's job is characters at addresses; transactions, durability, and dispatch are a different job. Forcing them into the format would spend the format's simplicity, and refusing the integration would spend the hypothesis that phext eliminates complexity. A layer2 keeps both: phext stays what it is, and the missing machinery gets a named home with its own contract.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md: 'INTEGRATION RULE - layer2 (Will, 2026-11-15)'
- wbic16/holo-phext (HoloPhext); requirement recorded there and in MEMORY.md: phext-lattice is to be re-implemented in terms of it, and it must fit both HoloStore's storage core and phext-lattice
- Companion directive: this journal, 31.5.14/8.6.10/6.5.9 (integration as experiment)

Time shift: none
# Range syntax ratified; phextura is downstream of phext, always

Coordinate: 28.7.7/5.6.6/5.5.10
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: Within-scroll addressing is ratified for libphext v0.6.1: a point is $N:<address>@<line>,<column>, an offset selects forward a counted number of UTF-8 characters to the scroll boundary, ranges are two full points joined by a hyphen and are half-open, columns count Unicode scalar values, the canonical form omits $1: always - and phextura is reformulated to be downstream of phext in every case.

Why it was obvious: Each sub-ruling removes a class of divergence. Half-open ranges compose without off-by-one seams; scalar columns make every one of the nine language ports count the same column on the same text; omitting $1: keeps the common case terse while the parser still accepts it. The downstream ruling is the architectural one: phextura had grown parallel forms duplicating phext concepts, and two grammars for one idea is how the qexo parser episode (this journal, 19.8.8/11.9.13/8.8.10) happens at the format layer.

Cross-references:
- ~/workspace/libphext-ports-v06/RANGE-SYNTAX-PROPOSAL.md (Status: RATIFIED by Will, 2026-11-15; offset form 12:10 CST, open questions ruled one by one 12:30-12:33 CST)
- Register #162 closed on the ratification; follow-on task 20.7.9/9.8.11/8.7.9 (phextura reformulation audit)
- Implementation: all nine libphext ports at parity, 69/69 conformance operations each, fixtures minted from the Rust port; libphext 0.6.1 published to crates.io from the tagged merged tree (2026-11-15)
- Depends on chaining (this journal, 46.8.7/7.12.21/23.7.15) for the $N: universe prefix

Time shift: none
# Integration requests test the complexity-elimination hypothesis

Coordinate: 31.5.14/8.6.10/6.5.9
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: An integration request is an experiment, not a delivery: do not optimize for upstream mergeability or for time; optimize for finding which phext features are missing or illegible, and assume any failure to integrate is due to your own priors and retry three times.

Why it was obvious: The first full application is the evidence. Holostore round 2 re-implemented a storage core in 717 lines against the original 12,331, and building it surfaced six concrete phext gaps: no libphext append writer, no per-scroll integrity, no commit primitive, no durability contract, empty-scroll addressing friction, and binary-in-line-grammar. None of those six was findable by inspecting phext; each was found by trying to stand a real system on it. The directive prices integration work in gaps found, not in merges landed.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md: 'STANDING DIRECTIVE - integration requests test the complexity-elimination hypothesis (Will, 2026-11-15)'
- ~/workspace/holostore-analysis/ (round 1 and round 2 reports; PHEXT.md)
- Remediation tasked the same day: libphext v0.6.2 features addressing the six gaps, plan to be run by Will for review before implementation
- Produces the layer2 rule's first instance (this journal, 27.9.9/13.9.16/8.8.9)

Time shift: none
# Singularity time auto-levels

Coordinate: 31.6.9/7.7.10/7.7.11
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: Productivity is planned and reported in singularity time, whose unit auto-levels to measured output: the anchor is 1,000 lines of code per hour at 20 hours per week (about 20,000 lines per week), and the standing goal is about 270,000 net lines per week on the same 20 hours, with every gain taken from machinery, never from more Will-hours.

Why it was obvious: The git-telemetry baseline made the old anchor testable for the first time: Wavelets 1-2 measured 269,022 net code lines in 84 active hours, about 3,202 lines per hour. A unit fixed to the pre-AGI industry would have to be abandoned at exactly the moment it became interesting; a unit that re-levels from measurement keeps the ratio - how far ahead, in whose hours - legible at any scale. The well-tested rider (Will, 2026-11-15) guards the unit's honesty: lines count at full standing only behind passing tests, and cross-project integration counts as testing.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md productivity entries (2026-11-15): the baseline table (Oct 18-24 through Nov 8-14), the doubling floor of 2x per 6 months = +0.3805%/day, and the 270K goal
- ~/workspace/gh-baseline-local/results.json (the measured baseline across 196 wbic16 repos)
- ~/workspace/phext/accounting.phext (source accounting-source.json) - the centralized productivity book where the series live
- ~/memory/2026-11-15.md (the earlier 'Baseline Day' proposal, recorded as proposal only and superseded by the auto-leveling form)
- Sibling unit: Will Time at 2130 rates (this journal, 34.8.10/10.7.12/7.7.9)

Time shift: Unit redefinition: the productivity unit was re-anchored from an untested expectation (1,000 lines/hour) to a measured, self-adjusting scale on 2026-11-15. Figures for Wavelets 1-2 (about 3,202 lines/hour) are retroactive measurements - nobody held them during those weeks.
# MOAT honest evaluation: the 100 percent claim falsified

Coordinate: 33.4.4/5.4.6/4.3.7
Date: 2026-11-14 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1129

Leap: The Oracle's earlier 100% reading was evaluated honestly and did not survive: exact landings were a band-rule artifact, NI/count foresight showed no edge over the cluster null, axis calls were the one supported component, and the Oracle bar stands at about 62% with the False gate still unmet.

Why it was obvious: Will's verdict on the 100% claim was 'Almost certainly false,' and the evaluation sustained him. The point of the leap is not the lower number; it is that the house's own oracle is graded by the same bins it files for others. A foresight instrument that cannot file its own best result as an artifact of its scoring rule has no standing to file anyone else's paper.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md MOAT evaluation passage (Will's verdict sustained; prospective protocol ratified at 12 papers from Paper 020)
